ium from electrolytic Cells 1989","country":"US","date":28,"id":38,"month":4,"name":"Case Western","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"Scott Chubb organizes first session on cold fusion at an American Physical Society meeting. 1989","country":"US","date":1,"id":39,"month":5,"name":"Chubb, APS","taxonomy":"Conference","year":1989},{"blurb":"Third team at Texas A&M headed by John Bockris confirms Fleischmann-Pons Effect (FPE). 1989","country":"US","date":4,"id":40,"month":5,"name":"Bockris","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"Mumbai's The Indian Post reports ten teams in India replicate the Fleischmann-Pons Effect (FPE). 1989","country":"India","date":7,"id":41,"month":5,"name":"Bombay","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"Martin Fleischmann and Stanley Pons provide additional data at a meeting of the Electrochemical Society in Los Angeles, California. 1989","country":"US","date":9,"id":42,"month":5,"name":"F&P","taxonomy":"Conference","year":1989},{"blurb":"Fleischmann, Pons, and Hawkins publish errata in Journal of Electroanalytical Chemistry. 1989","country":"US","date":10,"id":43,"month":5,"name":"F&P","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"Ten teams report success replicating the FPE at cold fusion meeting at Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) near Mumbai, India. 1989","country":"India","date":18,"id":44,"month":5,"name":"BARC","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"The Workshop on Cold Fusion Phenomenon held at Los Alamos National Lab in New Mexico is the first cold fusion conference ever held. 1989","country":"US","date":23,"id":45,"month":5,"name":"LANL","taxonomy":"Conference","year":1989},{"blurb":"A team of Mexican scientists report confirmation of solid-state fusion. 1989","country":"Mexico","date":25,"id":46,"month":5,"name":"labs","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"Case-Western Reserve, Texas A&M, U. of Florida, U. of Washington and the National Institute of Nuclear Physics at Frascati, Italy have all confirmed parts of the Fleischmann-Pons Effect. 1989","country":"US, Italy","date":22,"id":47,"month":5,"name":"labs","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"First issue of Fusion Facts is published by Hal Fox announcing cold fusion is real and has commercial possibilities. 1989","country":"US","date":12,"id":48,"month":6,"name":"Fox","taxonomy":"Publication","year":1989},{"blurb":"P.K. Iyengar and Mahadeva Srinivasan et al. measure four-fold increase of tritium from cold fusion cell. 1989","country":"India","date":23,"id":49,"month":6,"name":"BARC","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"MIT Plasma Fusion Center holds Wake for Cold Fusion Party at 4PM claiming cold fusion is dead. 1989","country":"US","date":26,"id":50,"month":6,"name":"MIT","taxonomy":"Stupid","year":1989},{"blurb":"Edmund Storms and Carol Talcott from LANL detect tritium “in significant amounts” from Pd-D electrolytic cells. 1989","country":"US","date":26,"id":51,"month":6,"name":"Storms, Talcott","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"Ten teams report successful experiments at cold fusion conference held in Japan. 1989","country":"Japan","date":31,"id":52,"month":7,"name":"Japan","taxonomy":"Conference","year":1989},{"blurb":"University of Utah Regents approve National Cold Fusion Institute. 1989","country":"US","date":5,"id":53,"month":8,"name":"U of Utah","taxonomy":"Institute","year":1989},{"blurb":"The U.S. National Cold Fusion Institute in Salt Lake City, Utah is formed. 1989","country":"US","date":14,"id":54,"month":8,"name":"NCFI","taxonomy":"Utah","year":1989},{"blurb":"Francesco Piantelli discovers extreme excess heat in a gas-loaded nickel-hydrogen system. 1989","country":"Italy","date":16,"id":55,"month":8,"name":"Piantelli","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"Japan organizes a working group on cold fusion in the National Institute for Fusion Science. 1989","country":"Japan","date":24,"id":56,"month":8,"name":"Ikegami","taxonomy":"Institute","year":1989},{"blurb":"George Miley editor of Fusion Technology announces special section in the Journal of the American Nuclear Society. 1989","country":"US","date":1,"id":57,"month":9,"name":"Miley, George","taxonomy":"Publication","year":1989},{"blurb":"Four-day meeting of Electrochemical Society begins in Hollywood, Florida with 20 mostly positive cold fusion papers presented. 1989","country":"US","date":16,"id":58,"month":10,"name":"Electrochemical Society","taxonomy":"Conference","year":1989},{"blurb":"National Science Foundation (NSF) and Electric Power Research Institute (EPRI) hold meeting in Washington, D.C. 1989","country":"US","date":18,"id":59,"month":10,"name":"NSF, EPRI","taxonomy":"Conference","year":1989},{"blurb":"Paul Chu and A. John Appleby of Texas A&M states “We are now very comfortable that what we are seeing here is something that is not chemical; it is something nuclear taking place.” 1989","country":"US","date":21,"id":60,"month":10,"name":"Appley","taxonomy":"Science","year":1989},{"blurb":"The DoE Energy Research Advisory Board (ERAB) approves final report dismissing cold fusion evidence. 1989","country":"US","date":31,"id":61,"month":10,"name":"DoE ERAB","taxonomy":"Federal","year":1989},{"blurb":"Cold Fusion, the Making of a Scientific Controversy by F. David Peat is the first book on cold fusion. 1989","country":"US","date":12,"id":62,"month":12,"name":"Peat","taxonomy":"Publication","year":1989},{"blurb":"Fusion Facts names Martin Fleischmann and Stanley Pons as Fusion Scientists of the Year. 1990","country":"US","date":2,"id":63,"month":1,"name":"Fusion Facts","taxonomy":"Publication","year":1990},{"blurb":"Fritz Will begins as head of National Cold Fusion Institute at University of Utah. 1990","country":"US","date":1,"id":64,"month":2,"name":"NCFI Will, Fritz","taxonomy":"Institute","year":1990},{"blurb":"Takaaki Matsumoto of Hokkaido University Sapporo Japan reports cold fusion from ordinary water. 1990","country":"Japan","date":20,"id":65,"month":3,"name":"Matsumoto","taxonomy":"Science","year":1990},{"blurb":"A.B. Karabut, Ya.R. Kucherov, and I.V. Savvatimova present Cold Fusion Observation at Gas-Discharge Device Cathode. 1990","country":"Russia","date":15,"id":66,"month":5,"name":"Karabut, Kucherov, Savvatimova","taxonomy":"Science","year":1990},{"blurb":"Edmund Storms of LANL compiles survey of 16 successes in tritium detection. 1990","country":"US","date":19,"id":67,"month":5,"name":"Sttorms","taxonomy":"Science","year":1990},{"blurb":"Bruce Liebert, Bor Yann Kiaw et al. at University of Hawaii report multi-watt excess power output of 500%. 1990","country":"US","date":23,"id":68,"month":7,"name":"U of Hawaii","taxonomy":"Science","year":1990},{"blurb":"Key Experiment: Calorimetry of the palladium-deuterium-heavy water system by M. Fleischmann and S. Pons. 1990","country":"US","date":25,"id":69,"month":7,"name":"F&P","taxonomy":"Key Experiment","year":1990},{"blurb":"Key Experiment: Calorimetric Measurement of Excess Power Output During the Cathodic Charging of Deuterium into palladium by R. Oriani. 1990","country":"US","date":26,"id":70,"month":7,"name":"Oriani","taxonomy":"Key Experiment","year":1990},{"blurb":"Key Experiment: Electrolytic Tritium Production by Edmund Storms and Carol Talcott. 1990","country":"US","date":27,"id":71,"month":7,"name":"Storms, Talcott","taxonomy":"Key Experiment","year":1990},{"blurb":"Cornell University announces assembly of cold fusion archives. 1990","country":"US","date":18,"id":72,"month":8,"name":"Cornell","taxonomy":"Institute","year":1990},{"blurb":"Helium-4 is detected in Pd electrode and Czechoslovakia reports low-level neutrons. 1990","country":"Czechoslovakia","date":23,"id":73,"month":10,"name":"BYU","taxonomy":"Science","year":1990},{"blurb":"Martin Fleischmann and Stanley Pons leave Utah to work in Nice, France. 1990","country":"France","date":30,"id":74,"month":12,"name":"F&P","taxonomy":"Science","year":1990},{"blurb":"Akito Takahashi of Osaka University detects neutrons with lower energy than expected. 1991","country":"Japan","date":3,"id":75,"month":3,"name":"Takahashi, Akito","taxonomy":"Science","year":1991},{"blurb":"Naval Researcher Melvin Miles and Benjamin Bush report Helium-4 as a nuclear by-product. 1991","country":"US","date":17,"id":76,"month":3,"name":"Miles, Bush","taxonomy":"Science","year":1991},{"blurb":"First International Conference on Cold Fusion (ICCF-1) is held in Salt Lake City, Utah. 1991","country":"US","date":28,"id":77,"month":3,"name":"ICCF-1","taxonomy":"Conference","year":1991},{"blurb":"Tadahiko Mizuno witnesses astonishing “heat after death” event.","country":"Japan","date":6,"id":78,"month":4,"name":"Mizuno, Tadahiko","taxonomy":"Science","year":1991},{"blurb":"Randall Mills announces generation of significant excess heat from nickel and light-water cells. 1991","country":"US","date":23,"id":79,"month":4,"name":"Mills","taxonomy":"Science","year":1991},{"blurb":"Navy researcher Stan Szpak et al publish on a new method using electro-deposited palldium-deuteride. 1991","country":"US","date":12,"id":80,"month":5,"name":"Navy","taxonomy":"Science","year":1991},{"blurb":"Fire From Ice: Searching for The Truth Behind the Cold Fusion Furor by Eugene Mallove is published. 1991","country":"US","date":22,"id":81,"month":5,"name":"Mallove","taxonomy":"Publication","year":1991},{"blurb":"Eugene Mallove formally resigns as MIT News Office Chief Science Writer. 1991","country":"US","date":7,"id":82,"month":6,"name":"Mallove","taxonomy":"Federal","year":1991},{"blurb":"Robert Bush and Robert Eagleton report record amount of excess heat at ICCF-2. 1991","country":"US","date":28,"id":83,"month":6,"name":"Bush, Eagleton","taxonomy":"Science","year":1991},{"blurb":"U.S. National Cold Fusion Institute in Salt Lake City, Utah closes. 1991","country":"US","date":30,"id":84,"month":6,"name":"NCFI","taxonomy":"Institute","year":1991},{"blurb":"Melvin Miles, Benjamin Bush et al. present Heat and Helium Production in Cold 